# test/MUTATION_COVERAGE - how many planted faults this suite must reject
#
# WHY A NUMBER IN A FILE
#
# Every assertion in test/shell is paired with a mutation proof: plant one fault
# in a throwaway copy of the toolkit, show the same assertion goes red, throw
# the copy away. That discipline is the reason a green suite here means
# something. It has one hole, and it is structural.
#
# DELETE A PROOF AND NOTHING GOES RED. The suite prints one fewer `ok` line and
# exits 0. test/run.sh keys on exit status, so a guard can lose the only
# evidence it can fail, and the run stays green with a slightly smaller number
# in a line nobody diffs. That is the same shape as the defect the proofs exist
# to catch, one level up - and this repository has already shipped it once, in a
# gate that graded from a counter every rebuilding command silently reset.
#
# So the counts are declared HERE, in a file whose whole content is the claim,
# and asserted by test/run.sh against what each suite actually rejected. A count
# that does not match is red IN BOTH DIRECTIONS:
#
#   fewer than declared   a proof was dropped. Something is now unproven, and
#                         nothing else in the suite would have said so.
#   more than declared    a proof was added and this file was not updated. Not a
#                         defect - an unreviewed change to what the suite claims
#                         to prove, and bumping the number is the thirty seconds
#                         in which somebody reads the new case.
#
# THIS FILE IS NOT A BUDGET THAT RATCHETS ITSELF. Nothing edits it
# automatically, and that is deliberate: a floor the flow raises to whatever
# today's run produced records history, not intent.
#
# A suite that did not run at all (no tclsh, absent fixture) is NOT compared -
# it is already reported by run.sh's hole gate, and failing it twice for one
# cause would train the reader to ignore both.
#
# A proof is counted by its assertion id: an id containing `.mutation` is a
# planted fault the suite rejected. That is the runtime count, not a grep of the
# source, because three of these suites plant their faults inside loops.
#
# Format:  <suite name>  <count>
